README.style
------------

Hackedbox Styles
================

A Hackedbox style is an X resource file containing the visual configuration
used by the window manager. Styles control the appearance of menus, window
decorations, fonts, colors, textures, borders, and the root background.

Styles are plain text files and can be created or modified with any text
editor.


Style Locations
===============

System-installed styles are located in:

    /usr/share/hackedbox/styles/

User styles may be placed in:

    ~/.hackedbox/styles/

A style may also be stored elsewhere on the filesystem. Hackedbox only
requires read access to the file.


Loading a Style
===============

The current style is selected through the Hackedbox menu or configuration.

When a style is loaded, Hackedbox reads the X resources contained in the
style file and applies the settings to the window manager.

After changing a style file, Hackedbox can be reconfigured from the menu.
The reconfigure operation causes Hackedbox to reload its configuration and
style resources without restarting the window manager or rebuilding
Hackedbox.

This makes it possible to edit a style, save the changes, and use the
Hackedbox menu's reconfigure command to apply the new appearance to the
running session.

A style change does not require rebuilding Hackedbox.

If a resource appears to have no effect after reconfiguring, verify the
resource name and value against the resources supported by the current
Hackedbox build. Resources that are not used by the component being
configured have no effect.## README.style

# Hackedbox Styles

A Hackedbox style is an X resource file containing the visual configuration
used by the window manager. Styles control the appearance of menus, window
decorations, fonts, colors, textures, borders, and the root background.

Styles are plain text files and can be created or modified with any text
editor.

# Style Locations

System-installed styles are located in:

```
/usr/share/hackedbox/styles/
```

User styles may be placed in:

```
~/.hackedbox/styles/
```

A style may also be stored elsewhere on the filesystem. Hackedbox only
requires read access to the file.

# Loading a Style

The current style is selected through the Hackedbox menu or configuration.

When a style is loaded, Hackedbox reads the X resources contained in the
style file and applies the settings to the window manager.

Styles can be changed without rebuilding Hackedbox.

# Style File Structure

A style consists of resource keys and values:

```
resource.key: value
```

Resource names are separated by periods.

Comments may begin with:

```
#
```

For example:

```
# Menu title
menu.title.font: Hack-14
menu.title.textColor: #FFFFFF
```

Resources that are not specified retain their default values.

# Fonts

Fonts are assigned to individual style components.

Examples:

```
menu.title.font: Hack-14
menu.frame.font: Hack-12
menu.clock.font: Hack-13
menu.date.font: Hack-13
```

Use a font name or alias supported by the X11 font system used by the
current Hackedbox build.

Different components may use different families and sizes.

# Root Background

Hackedbox uses hbsetbg for root background handling.

The current background resources are:

```
root.background
root.background.mode
```

For example:

```
root.background: /usr/share/hackedbox/backgrounds/default.jpg
root.background.mode: center
```

Supported background modes depend on the hbsetbg implementation.

User backgrounds may be stored under:

```
~/.hackedbox/backgrounds/
```

Background rotation can be configured with:

```
root.background.folder
root.background.timer
```

For example:

```
root.background.folder: ~/.hackedbox/backgrounds
root.background.timer: 60000
```

A timer value of 0 disables background rotation.

The older:

```
rootCommand:
```

resource is retained for compatibility with older styles but is not used by
the current root background system. New styles should use the
root.background resources instead.

# Menus

The Hackedbox menu system is divided into the title, frame, highlighted
item, clock, and date components.

Menu title resources:

```
menu.title.font
menu.title
menu.title.color
menu.title.colorTo
menu.title.textColor
menu.title.justify
```

Menu frame resources:

```
menu.frame.font
menu.frame
menu.frame.color
menu.frame.colorTo
menu.frame.textColor
menu.frame.disableColor
menu.frame.justify
```

Menu highlight resources:

```
menu.hilite
menu.hilite.color
menu.hilite.colorTo
menu.hilite.textColor
```

The title and frame can use different fonts, textures, colors, and text
justification.

# Menu Clock

The menu clock is a separate menu item controlled by style resources.

Clock resources:

```
menu.clock.font
menu.clock.textColor
menu.clock.format
menu.clock.justify
```

For example:

```
menu.clock.font: Hack-13
menu.clock.textColor: #AEB8C2
menu.clock.format: %I:%M:%S %p
menu.clock.justify: center
```

The clock format is passed to the standard C strftime() function.

Common format sequences include:

```
%H    hour, 00-23
%I    hour, 01-12
%M    minute
%S    second
%p    AM or PM
```

For example:

```
menu.clock.format: %I:%M:%S %p
```

displays:

```
03:42:17 PM
```

# Menu Date

The menu date is a separate menu item controlled by style resources.

Date resources:

```
menu.date.font
menu.date.textColor
menu.date.format
menu.date.justify
```

For example:

```
menu.date.font: Hack-13
menu.date.textColor: #AEB8C2
menu.date.format: %m/%d/%Y
menu.date.justify: center
```

The date format is passed to the standard C strftime() function.

Common format sequences include:

```
%m    month, 01-12
%d    day of month, 01-31
%Y    four-digit year
```

For example:

```
menu.date.format: %m/%d/%Y
```

displays:

```
09/19/2026
```

The clock and date fonts, colors, formats, and justification are independent
of one another.

# Menu Justification

Menu title, frame, clock, and date text can each have their own
justification.

Resources:

```
menu.title.justify
menu.frame.justify
menu.clock.justify
menu.date.justify
```

Supported values are:

```
left
center
right
```

For example:

```
menu.frame.justify: center
menu.clock.justify: center
menu.date.justify: center
```

# Menu Bullets

Menu bullets are configured independently from menu icons.

Resources:

```
menu.bullet
menu.bullet.position
```

Supported bullet values are:

```
empty
triangle
diamond
square
```

The bullet position can be:

```
left
right
```

For example:

```
menu.bullet: triangle
menu.bullet.position: right
```

The bullet setting is not a boolean value.

# Menu Icons

Menu item icons can be enabled or disabled with:

```
menu.icon
```

The icon resource is a boolean value.

Supported values include:

```
true
false
on
off
yes
no
```

For example:

```
menu.icon: true
```

or:

```
menu.icon: off
```

Icon placement is controlled independently with:

```
menu.icon.position
```

Supported positions are:

```
left
right
```

For example:

```
menu.icon: true
menu.icon.position: left
```

or:

```
menu.icon: true
menu.icon.position: right
```

The icon setting controls whether menu icons are displayed. The position
setting controls which side of the menu item is reserved for and displays
the icon.

Menu icons are associated with individual menu entries. A menu entry must
have an icon assigned before an icon can be displayed.

# Textures

Textures determine how a component is drawn.

Texture descriptions can specify:

```
raised
sunken
flat
```

along with:

```
solid
gradient
```

Gradient direction can also be specified.

Common gradient directions include:

```
vertical
diagonal
crossdiagonal
```

Examples:

```
menu.title: raised gradient diagonal
menu.frame: sunken gradient diagonal
menu.hilite: raised gradient diagonal
```

A solid texture uses its primary color:

```
menu.frame: flat solid
menu.frame.color: #000000
```

A gradient uses both:

```
color
colorTo
```

For example:

```
menu.title: raised gradient diagonal
menu.title.color: #5A5E62
menu.title.colorTo: #14181C
```

Texture keywords are not required to use uppercase characters.

# Colors

Colors may be specified using standard X color names or hexadecimal/X color
specifications.

Examples:

```
white
black
#FFFFFF
#000000
#5A5E62
```

Hackedbox styles may use six-digit hexadecimal RGB colors:

```
#RRGGBB
```

For example:

```
#5A5E62
#0A141E
#46505A
```

Gradient textures use:

```
color
colorTo
```

Solid textures normally require only the primary:

```
color
```

Text colors are specified through the appropriate textColor resource.

For example:

```
menu.frame.textColor: #5A6470
menu.clock.textColor: #AEB8C2
menu.date.textColor: #AEB8C2
```

# Window Decorations

Window decorations have separate focused and unfocused states.

The title, label, buttons, frame, handle, and grip can each have their own
appearance and colors.

Window title resources:

```
window.title.focus
window.title.focus.color
window.title.focus.colorTo

window.title.unfocus
window.title.unfocus.color
window.title.unfocus.colorTo
```

Window label resources:

```
window.label.focus
window.label.focus.color
window.label.focus.colorTo
window.label.focus.textColor

window.label.unfocus
window.label.unfocus.color
window.label.unfocus.colorTo
window.label.unfocus.textColor
```

Window button resources:

```
window.button.focus
window.button.focus.color
window.button.focus.colorTo
window.button.focus.picColor

window.button.unfocus
window.button.unfocus.color
window.button.unfocus.colorTo
window.button.unfocus.picColor

window.button.pressed
window.button.pressed.color
window.button.pressed.colorTo
```

Window frame resources:

```
window.frame.focusColor
window.frame.unfocusColor
```

Window handle resources:

```
window.handle.focus
window.handle.focus.color
window.handle.focus.colorTo

window.handle.unfocus
window.handle.unfocus.color
window.handle.unfocus.colorTo
```

Window grip resources:

```
window.grip.focus
window.grip.focus.color
window.grip.focus.colorTo

window.grip.unfocus
window.grip.unfocus.color
window.grip.unfocus.colorTo
```

Button pressed resources provide the appearance used while a window button
is pressed.

# Window Layout and Justification

Window title and label text layout is controlled by:

```
window.justify
```

Supported values are:

```
left
center
right
```

For example:

```
window.justify: center
```

Menu components have their own independent justification resources.

# Borders and Geometry

The following resources control the physical dimensions of window
decorations:

```
borderWidth
bevelWidth
handleWidth
```

The border color is controlled by:

```
borderColor
```

For example:

```
borderColor: #000000
borderWidth: 1
bevelWidth: 2
handleWidth: 5
```

Bevel width controls the size of the three-dimensional edge shading.

Border width controls the width of the window border.

Handle width controls the height of the window resize handle.

# Pointers and Cursors

Mouse pointer handling is provided by X11 rather than by the Hackedbox
window decoration and menu texture system.

Cursor themes and sizes can therefore be configured through X cursor
resources, for example:

```
Xcursor.theme: breeze_cursors
Xcursor.size: 26
```

These settings normally belong in the user's X resource configuration
rather than in the Hackedbox style.

# Creating a Style From Scratch

The simplest way to create a new style is to start with an empty file and
add only the resources that need to be changed.

For example:

```
# My Hackedbox style

root.background: /usr/share/hackedbox/backgrounds/default.jpg
root.background.mode: center

menu.title.font: Hack-14
menu.title: raised gradient diagonal
menu.title.color: #5A5E62
menu.title.colorTo: #14181C
menu.title.textColor: #FFFFFF
menu.title.justify: center

menu.frame.font: Hack-12
menu.frame: sunken gradient diagonal
menu.frame.color: #0A141E
menu.frame.colorTo: #46505A
menu.frame.textColor: #5A6470
menu.frame.justify: center

menu.hilite: raised gradient diagonal
menu.hilite.color: #5A5E62
menu.hilite.colorTo: #14181C
menu.hilite.textColor: #FFFFFF

menu.clock.font: Hack-13
menu.clock.textColor: #AEB8C2
menu.clock.format: %I:%M:%S %p
menu.clock.justify: center

menu.date.font: Hack-13
menu.date.textColor: #AEB8C2
menu.date.format: %m/%d/%Y
menu.date.justify: center

menu.bullet: triangle
menu.bullet.position: right

menu.icon: true
menu.icon.position: left

window.justify: center

borderColor: #000000
bevelWidth: 2
borderWidth: 1
handleWidth: 5
```

Resources that are not specified retain their default values.

This makes it possible to build a style incrementally instead of copying
an existing style and modifying every setting.

# Installing a User Style

Save the style in:

```
~/.hackedbox/styles/
```

For example:

```
~/.hackedbox/styles/MyStyle
```

The style can then be selected through the Hackedbox menu.

System-wide styles installed by Hackedbox are normally located in:

```
/usr/share/hackedbox/styles/
```

# Reconfiguring

After modifying a style, reload or reconfigure Hackedbox so that the new
resources are read.

If a resource appears to have no effect, verify the resource name and value
against the resources supported by the current Hackedbox build.

Resources that are not used by the component being configured have no
effect.

A style change does not require rebuilding Hackedbox.

